\title{Analytic Continuation as a General Mechanism\\for Transform Uncertainty}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 18, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Uncertainty principles are often identified with the variance inequality for quantum position and momentum, but their mathematical source is the duality created by integral transforms. We develop a self-contained qualitative theory for transforms whose outputs admit holomorphic continuation. A holomorphic transform output on a connected complex domain cannot vanish on a nonempty open subset without vanishing identically. For entire outputs, compact support is impossible for a nonzero function; the zero set of a nonzero entire function has planar Lebesgue measure zero; and its nonzero set has infinite measure. Positive-measure vanishing and finite-measure support therefore force an entire function to be zero. The framework applies to compactly supported Fourier inputs through entire continuation, to Laplace transforms on right half-planes, and to Mellin transforms on vertical strips. Sine, sinc, cosine, and Gaussian examples clarify the distinction between exact support and rapid decay. A Hilbert-space Gram inequality supplies a finite-dimensional quantitative companion. We identify the boundary of the framework: invertibility alone is insufficient, and Radon uncertainty requires geometric range information rather than one-variable holomorphy.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
The Heisenberg relation
\[
\Delta x\,\Delta p\geq \frac{\hbar}{2}
\]
is physical in its interpretation and scale, but Fourier analytic in its mathematical architecture. Momentum is proportional to spatial frequency, and localization of a function competes with localization of its Fourier transform. Related tradeoffs occur in spectroscopy, signal processing, tomography, sparse approximation, and learned representations.

Uncertainty has several forms. Variance principles compare second moments; entropic principles compare information measures; support principles ask whether a function and its transform can both be exactly confined. We study a qualitative support mechanism available whenever the transformed signal extends holomorphically to a connected complex domain.

The mechanism is the identity principle. A smooth real function can vanish on an interval and be nonzero elsewhere. A holomorphic function cannot: vanishing on a nonempty open part of a connected domain forces global vanishing. Unless an entire function is identically zero, its zeros are isolated. Its zero set is consequently countable and has planar measure zero, while its nonzero set has infinite measure.

This yields a transform-independent principle under explicit hypotheses. If a transform maps an input class to holomorphic functions on a connected domain, no nonzero output can contain an open gap. If the transform is injective, global output vanishing implies input vanishing. Fourier transforms of compactly supported integrable functions extend to entire functions; Laplace transforms are holomorphic on half-planes; and Mellin transforms are holomorphic on vertical strips.

Invertibility alone does not suffice: the identity operator is invertible and preserves compact support. The operative conditions are analytic continuation and injectivity. Radon transforms mark a different boundary, because their support theory depends on projection geometry and the Fourier-slice theorem.

\section{Definitions and analytic background}
Let $f:X\to\mathbb C$. Its nonzero set and topological support are
\[
\nz(f)=\{x\in X:f(x)\neq0\},\qquad
\supp_{\rm top}(f)=\overline{\nz(f)}.
\]
The function has compact support when $\supp_{\rm top}(f)$ is compact. We identify $\mathbb C$ with $\mathbb R^2$ and denote planar Lebesgue measure by $m_2$.

A function on an open set $U\subseteq\mathbb C$ is holomorphic if it is complex differentiable throughout $U$, and entire if $U=\mathbb C$. A domain is a nonempty connected open set.

\begin{lemma}[Identity principle]\label{lem:identity}
Let $U\subseteq\mathbb C$ be connected and open, and let $F:U\to\mathbb C$ be holomorphic. If a nonempty open set $W\subseteq U$ satisfies $F|_W=0$, then $F=0$ throughout $U$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
At any point of $W$, every derivative vanishes, so the local power series is zero. Equivalently, the zeros of a nonzero holomorphic function are isolated. Analytic continuation and connectedness propagate the zero germ throughout $U$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Analytic transform class]
A pair $(T,\mathcal A)$ is an analytic transform class on a domain $U$ if every $f\in\mathcal A$ has an output $Tf$ admitting a holomorphic representative on $U$. It is \emph{faithful} if $T$ is injective on $\mathcal A$.
\end{definition}
Holomorphy prohibits open regions of exact zeros in the output; faithfulness transfers global output vanishing back to the input.

\section{Main uncertainty results}
\begin{theorem}[Identity-principle uncertainty]\label{thm:general}
Let $F$ be holomorphic on a connected open set $U\subseteq\mathbb C$. If $F$ vanishes on a nonempty open subset of $U$, then $F$ vanishes throughout $U$. Consequently, in a faithful analytic transform class, no nonzero input can have an output that vanishes on a nonempty open subset of its analytic domain.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The first assertion is Lemma~\ref{lem:identity}. The resulting identity $Tf=0$, combined with injectivity, gives $f=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Compact-support obstruction]\label{thm:compact}
If $F:\mathbb C\to\mathbb C$ is entire and has compact topological support, then $F\equiv0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The complement of the compact support is nonempty because $\mathbb C$ is not compact. It is open, and $F$ vanishes there. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:general} on $\mathbb C$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Isolation of zeros]
If a holomorphic function $F$ on a domain is not identically zero, every zero of $F$ is isolated.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
At a zero $z_0$, choose the least nonzero coefficient in the power series. Then $F(z)=(z-z_0)^nG(z)$ with $G(z_0)\neq0$. Continuity makes $G$ nonzero near $z_0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Countability of discrete planar sets]
Every discrete subset of $\mathbb C$ is countable.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The plane has a countable basis of disks with rational data. Assign to each point a basis disk containing it and no other point of the discrete set.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Zero-set measure theorem]\label{thm:zeros}
If $F$ is a nonzero entire function, then
\[
m_2\{z\in\mathbb C:F(z)=0\}=0.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The zero set is discrete, hence countable. Countable subsets of the plane are null.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Infinite-support-measure theorem]\label{thm:infinite}
If $F$ is a nonzero entire function, then
\[
m_2\{z\in\mathbb C:F(z)\neq0\}=\infty.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The zero set is null by Theorem~\ref{thm:zeros}. Since the plane has infinite measure, its complement has infinite measure.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Positive-measure vanishing]
An entire function that vanishes on a set of positive planar measure is identically zero.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Otherwise Theorem~\ref{thm:zeros} makes its zero set null, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Finite-measure nonzero set]
An entire function whose nonzero set has finite planar measure is identically zero.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Otherwise Theorem~\ref{thm:infinite} gives infinite measure.
\end{proof}

These statements are stronger than compact-support obstruction for an entire function. They do not, by themselves, prove the full finite-measure Fourier support theorem for arbitrary real-line signals: the entire-output assumption is substantial and commonly comes from compact input support.

\section{Fourier specialization}
For $f\in L^1(\mathbb R)$, use
\[
\widehat f(k)=\int_{\mathbb R}f(x)e^{-ikx}\,dx.
\]
If $f$ is supported in $[-R,R]$, then
\[
F(z)=\int_{-R}^{R}f(x)e^{-izx}\,dx,
\qquad
F^{(n)}(z)=\int_{-R}^{R}(-ix)^nf(x)e^{-izx}\,dx.
\]
Uniform domination on compact subsets of the $z$-plane permits differentiation under the integral, so $F$ is entire and restricts to $\widehat f$ on the real axis.

\begin{theorem}[Fourier compact-support uncertainty]
Let $f\in L^1(\mathbb R)$ have compact support. If the entire continuation $F$ of $\widehat f$ has compact support in $\mathbb C$, or if its nonzero set has finite planar measure, then $F\equiv0$ and $f=0$ almost everywhere.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:compact} or the finite-measure corollary, followed by injectivity of the Fourier transform on $L^1(\mathbb R)$.
\end{proof}

For the box $f=\mathbf1_{[-1,1]}$,
\[
\widehat f(k)=
\begin{cases}2\sin(k)/k,&k\neq0,\\2,&k=0.\end{cases}
\]
The removable singularity gives an entire continuation. Its separated zeros do not confine it: sinc has an unbounded tail and is nonzero almost everywhere on the real axis. The entire functions $\sin z$ and $\cos z$ are nontrivial, so their complex zero sets are null and their nonzero sets have infinite planar measure.

For $a>0$, the Gaussian satisfies
\[
f_a(x)=e^{-ax^2},\qquad
\widehat f_a(k)=\sqrt{\frac\pi a}\,e^{-k^2/(4a)}.
\]
Both sides have full support and rapid decay. The complex Gaussian $e^{-z^2}$ is entire and nowhere zero, because the exponential has no zeros; its support is all of $\mathbb C$. Under unitary normalization, Gaussians attain equality in variance uncertainty. Optimal localization therefore means balanced tails, not double compactness.

\section{Laplace half-plane uncertainty}
For suitable $f:[0,\infty)\to\mathbb C$,
\[
\mathcal Lf(s)=\int_0^\infty f(t)e^{-st}\,dt
\]
is holomorphic on a right half-plane $H_c=\{s:\RePart s>c\}$. Such a half-plane is convex and connected.

\begin{theorem}[Laplace open-gap uncertainty]
Let $F$ be holomorphic on $H_c$. If $F$ vanishes on a nonempty open subset of $H_c$, then $F=0$ throughout $H_c$. If $F=\mathcal Lf$ in an injective input class, then $f=0$ almost everywhere.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:general} to the connected half-plane and then use injectivity.
\end{proof}

For $f(t)=e^{-t}\mathbf1_{[0,\infty)}(t)$,
\[
\mathcal Lf(s)=\frac1{s+1},\qquad \RePart s>-1.
\]
It decays as $|s|$ grows but never vanishes in its domain. This distinguishes smallness from exact silence.

\section{Mellin strip uncertainty}
The Mellin transform is
\[
\mathcal Mf(s)=\int_0^\infty f(x)x^{s-1}\,dx.
\]
Its convergence domain is often a vertical strip $S_{a,b}=\{s:a<\RePart s<b\}$, an intersection of half-planes and hence convex and connected.

\begin{theorem}[Mellin strip uncertainty]
Let $F$ be holomorphic on $S_{a,b}$. If $F$ vanishes on a nonempty open subset of the strip, then it vanishes throughout the strip. If $F=\mathcal Mf$ in an injective class, then $f=0$ almost everywhere.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:general} on the connected strip and then injectivity.
\end{proof}

The substitution $x=e^u$ gives
\[
\mathcal Mf(\sigma+i\omega)
=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}f(e^u)e^{\sigma u}e^{i\omega u}\,du,
\]
a Fourier transform in logarithmic position. Multiplicative scaling in $x$ becomes translation in $u$. For $f(x)=e^{-x}$, one obtains $\mathcal Mf(s)=\Gamma(s)$ on $\RePart s>0$; the gamma function has no zeros.

\section{Quantitative Hilbert-space companion}
\begin{theorem}[Signal--probe Gram inequality]
In a real inner-product space, any vectors $u,v$ satisfy
\[
0\leq \lVert u\rVert^2\lVert v\rVert^2-\langle u,v\rangle^2.
\]
Equivalently, $|\langle u,v\rangle|\leq\lVert u\rVert\lVert v\rVert$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For $v\neq0$, expand the nonnegative quantity
\[
\left\lVert u-\frac{\langle u,v\rangle}{\lVert v\rVert^2}v\right\rVert^2.
\]
After multiplication by $\lVert v\rVert^2$, the stated Gram determinant appears. The case $v=0$ is immediate.
\end{proof}

In variance arguments, $u$ and $v$ are centered position and frequency probes. Integration by parts or a commutator controls their inner product, and Gram positivity gives a product bound. Analytic continuation controls exact support; Hilbert geometry controls quantitative concentration. The mechanisms are complementary.

\section{Numerical algorithms and interpretation}
Numerics cannot establish exact support on an infinite domain. Finite windows, sampled grids, and floating-point thresholds measure concentration rather than literal support.

For a sampled Gaussian, a centered fast Fourier transform estimates
\[
\Delta x=\left(\int(x-\mu_x)^2|f(x)|^2dx\right)^{1/2},\quad
\Delta k=\left(\int(k-\mu_k)^2|\widehat f(k)|^2dk\right)^{1/2}.
\]
As the Gaussian width $\sigma$ varies, $\Delta x$ scales as $\sigma$ and $\Delta k$ as $\sigma^{-1}$, while their product remains approximately constant. An $N$-point FFT costs $O(N\log N)$ time and $O(N)$ memory. Sampling the exact box transform $2\sin(ak)/k$ displays sidelobes at arbitrarily large frequencies.

Laplace quadrature on complex points uses
\[
F(s)\approx\sum_{j=1}^N f(t_j)e^{-st_j}w_j.
\]
At $M$ complex points, direct evaluation costs $O(NM)$ time and can use $O(M)$ output memory. Comparing the transform of $e^{-t}$ with $1/(s+1)$ checks truncation and quadrature errors.

For Mellin computation, a uniform logarithmic grid turns each vertical line into a Fourier calculation. With $g_\sigma(u)=f(e^u)e^{\sigma u}$,
\[
\mathcal Mf(\sigma+i\omega)=\int g_\sigma(u)e^{i\omega u}\,du.
\]
An FFT computes sampled $\omega$ values in $O(N\log N)$ time. This is both numerically effective across scales and conceptually faithful to Mellin--Fourier duality.

\section{Applications and limitations}
If a learned integral transform is injective and its outputs have a common analytic continuation law, exact double sparsity is obstructed: a nonzero output cannot have an open zero patch, and an entire output cannot have a finite-measure nonzero set. Practical models should seek approximate sparsity and quantify leakage. Growth estimates and propagation-of-smallness theorems could supplement coherence-based uniqueness criteria.

In time-frequency design, compactly supported windows necessarily produce spectral tails, whereas Gaussian windows optimally balance variance. Mellin uncertainty similarly informs scale-equivariant systems, where narrow localization in log-position spreads Mellin frequency.

The Radon transform
\[
Rf(\theta,p)=\int_{x\cdot\theta=p}f(x)\,d\ell(x)
\]
requires separate treatment. The Fourier-slice theorem identifies the one-dimensional Fourier transform in $p$ with a radial restriction of the two-dimensional Fourier transform of $f$. Radon uncertainty couples spatial support, angular coverage, and slice frequency. One-variable holomorphy alone does not establish a general strip-support result; geometric range conditions are essential.

There are four principal limitations. First, invertibility alone produces no uncertainty theorem. Second, the finite-measure result here concerns entire outputs and is not the full finite-measure Fourier theorem for arbitrary real-line supports. Third, thresholds in computation do not certify zeros. Fourth, qualitative rigidity may be numerically unstable: an analytic function can be extremely small without vanishing.

\section{Future work}
Quantitative analytic continuation should replace exact vanishing by propagation-of-smallness bounds controlled by output growth and the measure of an observation set. Mellin transforms invite fractal uncertainty on multiplicatively porous sets after passage to logarithmic coordinates. Radon stability should connect strip width, angular coverage, and concentration in offset space. Analytic growth laws may provide uniqueness certificates for sparse learned transforms. Finally, Fourier restrictions along Radon slices may connect fractal uncertainty estimates to incidence bounds for tube families.

\section{Conclusion}
A common core of Fourier, Laplace, and Mellin support uncertainty is analytic continuation. A holomorphic function on a connected domain cannot vanish on an open patch without vanishing globally. A nonzero entire function has a null zero set and an infinite-measure nonzero set. These facts yield compact- and finite-support obstructions for analytic transform outputs.

The precise principle is not invertibility, but analytic rigidity together with injectivity. Other settings require other structure: Radon analysis needs geometry, while quantitative concentration uses Hilbert-space Gram positivity. Uncertainty is thus best understood as a family of structural limits imposed by transformations between representations.

